      "vulnerability": "Reentrancy via external call before state update",
      "severity": "critical",
      "description": "The contract makes an external call (msg.sender.call{value: amount}()) before updating the user's balance (balances[msg.sender] -= amount). This allows an attacker to recursively call withdraw() via a fallback function, draining the contract before the balance is decremented. This is the same pattern as the DAO hack (2016).",
      "evidence": ["static_analysis", "economic_modeling", "historical_patterns"],
      "confidence": 100
    },
    {
      "vulnerability": "Flash loan enabled reentrancy amplification",
      "severity": "critical",
      "description": "An attacker can take a flash loan of a large ETH amount, deposit it into the vault, then execute the reentrancy attack to drain the vault. The flash loan can be repaid with stolen funds, leaving the attacker with profit. This amplifies the impact to potentially millions of dollars.",
      "evidence": ["economic_modeling"],
      "confidence": 95
    },
    {
      "vulnerability": "MEV extraction via front-running deposits",
      "severity": "high",
      "description": "An attacker can monitor the mempool for large deposit transactions, front-run the deposit with their own deposit, then after the victim's deposit, execute the reentrancy attack to extract value from both deposits before the victim can withdraw.",
      "evidence": ["economic_modeling"],
      "confidence": 85
    },
    {
      "vulnerability": "Cross-contract composability with DeFi protocols",
      "severity": "high",
      "description": "An attacker can use a flash loan from Aave/dYdX, deposit into the vault, use the vault balance as collateral in a lending protocol, trigger reentrancy to drain the vault, and default on the loan with stolen funds. This leverages DeFi composability for 10x+ leverage.",
      "evidence": ["economic_modeling"],
      "confidence": 80
    }
  ],
  "overall_risk_score": 95
}

